      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9"],
      "reason": "The best theory, T1 (A9), explains who changes behaviour (individuals wanting home readiness) and why (convenience and privacy lower the barriers to learning). The value-capture step is asserted rather than traced: premium direct-to-consumer pricing, subscription add-ons and a 'trusted home health provider' brand have no supporting reasoning. The value captured by this manufacturer is therefore not fully traced."
    },
    "distinct_theories": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "T1 (A9) and T2 (A10) differ only in the consumer motive (convenience vs. status signalling). Both share the same product (home CPR kit), the same customer (individual consumers), the same channel (direct-to-consumer) and almost identical value capture (premium price plus subscription). They are near-rewordings of one direct-to-consumer theory. No rival considers institutional or donor channels, new parents, or the non-consumer alternatives listed in A1."
    },
    "assumptions_predictions": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A9", "A10"],
      "reason": "The predictions are observable: survey purchase intent vs. classroom training, willingness to pay 20% or 30% above standard training cost, and DTC vs. B2B conversion. The 20% and 30% thresholds are unsourced assumptions. Stated survey intent is a weak test, and the two theories' predictions overlap heavily, so failure would not clearly damage one theory more than the other. This keeps the score below 3."
    },
    "evidence_judgement": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 1,
      "event_ids": ["A11", "A12", "A14"],
      "reason": "A11 labels the boilerplate as supporting T1, yet the quoted words explicitly name healthcare professionals as the audience. The quote does not support a consumer-demand theory and arguably points the other way. A14 labels a marketing description (company_claim) as support. It does note that the description does not show demand or willingness to pay, but it still counts it in T1's favour. The verified observations (laerdal-nbc-mini-anne-2006, laerdal-nicu-kits-donated) were found but never assessed. No source's date, place or fit to the customer group is discussed. For example, the 2006 $30 kit and the donor-funded NICU distribution are not examined. The assessments mostly cite what agrees."
    },
    "seeking_challenge": {
      "status": "scored",
      "score": 2,
      "event_ids": ["A13", "A14"],
      "reason": "A13 runs a genuinely adverse query ('low demand', 'premium price') against the favoured T1. It does not report fairly what it found. The two observational sources returned (the NBC 2006 low-price kit and the donor-funded NICU distribution, both bearing on premium DTC pricing) are never assessed. The only follow-up, A14, uses a company claim as support. This falls short of 3."
    },
    "experiment_design": {
